      "writtenOn": "2021-07-20T20:36:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m sorry but could you remind me why the newer driver doesn\u0027t set c_cmd-\u003eresult on error? I think it should. Otherwise, we would need to do the translation everywhere. Am I missing something?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1e681015_882a39cf",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1390635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-21T14:32:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Here\u0027s where the result is dumped in the cros_ec-chardev driver: https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/third_party/kernel/next/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_chardev.c;l\u003d305. It looks like this has always been part of the driver, so I don\u0027t know why this just started causing issues recently. It may be related to the switch from cros_ec_cmd_xfer to cros_ec_cmd_xfer_status.",
      "parentUuid": "2e81b5dc_2af0f8f6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a9143f1f_f9550bed",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-22T23:31:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "IIUC, we should return -errno if r \u003c 0 or return -EECRESULT - result if r \u003d\u003d 0.\n\nI think we shouldn\u0027t translate errno to EC_RES_ (line 205 and 216) because existing ectool commands may behave unexpectedly. For example, a ectool command may think EC_RES_ERROR is from the EC but it could be actually EIO.",
      "parentUuid": "1e681015_882a39cf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ecf7d4da_fcf8393d",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1390635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-26T17:43:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ectool commands are behaving unexpectedly now (b/188956438). The change in cros_ec_proto.c to map result to Linux errno means we will never get r \u003d\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 msg-\u003eresult \u003d\u003d EC_RES_* here. \n\nOptions AFAICT\n1) Map errno to cros ec result (this CL)\n2) Roll back change to map result to Linux errno in cros_ec_proto.c\n3) Always retain result in cros_ec_chardev.c, even on error\n4) Refactor ectool to check for errno instead of \u0027-EECRESULT - result\u0027",
      "parentUuid": "a9143f1f_f9550bed",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7e23648d_0934c990",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132414
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-26T17:59:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How important is it for ectool that have the precise EC-side error codes? Would #4 lose a lot of useful precision?\n\nIt\u0027s pretty hard to both use standard errno codes *and* encode non-standard EC results (e.g., #3) -- we\u0027d have to figure out some convention for telling user space that despite the negative errno, we *did* pass it some other data.\n\n...which I think suggests either #1 or #2 are in order.\n\nWe could probably do #2 without totally reverting the kernel change: we could make the chardev ioctl() a special case.",
      "parentUuid": "ecf7d4da_fcf8393d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f2d60fa8_80fc9bed",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-26T18:53:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I believe ectool sub-commands need direct access to EC_RES_*. Direct access means EC_RES_ error code shouldn\u0027t be erased, generated, or translated by the kernel or comm-dev.c. IIUC, option 3 seems the right direction. This design allows EC engineers to mind only the EC host command implementation and ectool.",
      "parentUuid": "7e23648d_0934c990",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "92584bfd_03359fe6",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132414
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-26T19:01:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To be clear, it\u0027s unconventional to do #3 (i.e., both report an error *and* return data from kernel to user space). I guess if user space cooperates (by ensuring it zeroes out the result before the ioctl(), and then only trusting the result if it\u0027s non-zero -- for compatibility with older kernels) it could work...\n\nIs this on Rob to do, I guess?",
      "parentUuid": "f2d60fa8_80fc9bed",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "73f706eb_bbe4e5e0",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1390635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-27T17:24:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To clarify, the request is preserve the result on error here: https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/third_party/kernel/next/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_chardev.c;l\u003d305. Then return the result here as -EECRESULT - result.\n\nPseudo code:\nif (r \u003c 0) {\n  if (result !\u003d EC_COMMAND_RESULT_UNINITIALIZED)\n    return -EECRESULT - result;\n  return r;\n} else {\n  if (result \u003d\u003d EC_COMMAND_RESULT_UNINITIALIZED)\n    // Unexpected!!!\n    return -1;\n  if (result !\u003d EC_RES_SUCCESS)\n    return -EECRESULT - result;\n  return 0;\n}\n\nI can handle platform/ec changes. It\u0027d be helpful to get someone else to assist with the kernel changes.",
      "parentUuid": "92584bfd_03359fe6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "edc0248b_09726da1",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-28T01:17:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e it\u0027s unconventional to do #3 (i.e., both report an error *and* return data from kernel to user space)\n\nAnd we return only one value to a sub-command (i.e. -EECRESULT - result or r).\n\nIt\u0027s not clear to me why we need the kernel to interpret EC_RES_* (and make ioctl return r \u003c 0). For example, cros_ec_get_host_event_wake_mask does:\n\n\tret \u003d send_command(ec_dev, msg);\n\tif (ret \u003e\u003d 0) {\n\t\tif (msg-\u003eresult \u003d\u003d EC_RES_INVALID_COMMAND)\n\t\t\treturn -EOPNOTSUPP;\n\t\tif (msg-\u003eresult !\u003d EC_RES_SUCCESS)\n\t\t\treturn -EPROTO;\n\t}\n\nI feel it\u0027s not kernel\u0027s business. The kernel should focus on handling errors, which happen within the kernel (e.g. failure in resource allocation). IIUC, once send_command returns successfully, the kernel should return r \u003d 0. There is no more resource to allocate, right? Thus, there is no such case as r \u003c 0 AND result !\u003d EC_RES_UNINITIALIZED.\n\nI don\u0027t know everything about ioctl but am I missing anything?",
      "parentUuid": "73f706eb_bbe4e5e0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1dea8387_9a73271a",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132414
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-10T22:09:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I forgot this sat, and it\u0027s not clear who\u0027s supposed to guide this in general (I didn\u0027t write any patches that broke or fixed things here -- I\u0027m just reviewing):\n\n@Rob: you might need to ask someone in particular if you want their help. e.g., maybe Guenter can help partially undo his change. (See below.)\n\n@Daisuke: My understanding is that for the most part, the kernel changes were concerning in-kernel users -- for in-kernel users, we never want to expose EC error codes beyond the edges of the EC API. So for example, when a cros-ec RTC driver exposes errors to user space because we failed to program the alarm correctly, then we want a Linux error code, not an EC error code. There is no case where we care what the precise EC error code was. Most other cases are similar.\n\nYou found one counter-example: cros_ec_get_host_event_wake_mask().\n\n\u003e I don\u0027t know everything about ioctl but am I missing anything?\n\nWell, for one, ioctl() has always reported error here, so to do otherwise would be a breaking change. (As in, the Thou Shalt Not Break User Space kind.)\n\nIn the same vein, apparently this was a breaking change, and we didn\u0027t realize it:\nhttps://git.kernel.org/linus/0d080459e813ce8076f183cc73a4c9b64a39a4d8\nSo IMO, we should just partially revert it -- we can go back to the old non-standard error reporting mechanism, at least for ioctl().\n\nI don\u0027t really buy your \"resource allocation\" argument. The kernel abstracts lots of things away from its users, including the fact that there are tons of ways that an EC-based operation can fail even if the underlying transport succeeds. An error is an error -- we shouldn\u0027t say the ioctl() succeeded if the EC choked on it, IMO. (Similarly, we don\u0027t say that, for example, a WiFi operation succeeded just because the PCIe bus transmitted the operation -- if the chip firmware choked because the arguments are invalid, we\u0027d still want to report \"EINVAL\" or \"ERANGE\" or similar, as appropriate.)",
      "parentUuid": "edc0248b_09726da1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 201,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "3ccd1b2f34b5769eafb1b489e9cb6ea9edf728da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "112c4a51_292fc99d",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
